ont-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">Befor=
e judging it I want to name what's load-bearing, because the charter you've=
 invoked makes "what does a rule's content denote" the first question.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The thing you haven't stated: what do the two bitmasks mean? Both i=
ndex into the chart-as-stack-of-prior-rules, fine =E2=80=94 that closes the=
 self-reference cleanly and gives you a DAG that's acyclic by construction =
(zero-padding never lets an old rule point at a new one). But the <em>roles=
</em> of bitmask-1 vs bitmask-2 are unconstructed, and the plausible readin=
gs differ a lot in information content: (left child, right child) for a bin=
arized SPPF where each rule is one packed node; (left alternatives, right a=
lternatives) where a rule represents a symbol node with its full disjunctio=
n of children-pairs =E2=80=94 and here the Cartesian-product collapse is lo=
ssy and needs explicit justification, because (L=E2=82=81,R=E2=82=81) =E2=
=88=A8 (L=E2=82=82,R=E2=82=82) is not {L=E2=82=81,L=E2=82=82} =C3=97 {R=E2=
=82=81,R=E2=82=82}; (predecessors-in-derivation, successors-in-derivation) =
for a bidirectional pointer scheme; or something else. Until that recipe is=
 stated, "constructible" doesn't pass =E2=80=94 <code class=3D"bg-text-200/=
5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap r=
ounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">make_rule(b1, b2)</code> has the =
right shape but its meaning is what hash-consing identifies against.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">What does pass cleanly, conditional on the meaning being declared: =
the topological invariant is real and useful =E2=80=94 every rule reference=
s only earlier rules, the DAG is acyclic, you get a natural build order for=
 free. The hash-consing identity, if the intent is that trailing zeros don'=
t change a rule's identity (a rule added at N=3D10 with bits B is the <em>s=
ame</em> rule as one added at N=3D20 with B=E2=80=960=E2=80=A60), is quotie=
nt-safe =E2=80=94 but that equivalence wants to be declared, not inferred f=
rom the phrase "N gets extended with 0s." Otherwise you have a silent colla=
pse where the structure depends on N-at-time-of-insertion and the quotient-=
safety clause bites.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">On the operational side, separate from the semantic question: linea=
r search across the stack for an identical 2=C3=97N is O(R=C2=B7N) per inse=
rtion and O(R=C2=B3) over the full build, which won't scale. The standard m=
ove is a side-table <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-b=
order-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px t=
ext-[0.9rem]">dict[(packbits(left).tobytes(), packbits(right).tobytes())] =
=E2=86=92 index</code> with trailing zeros stripped before hashing, which a=
utomatically makes identity zero-extension-invariant =E2=80=94 the two desi=
gn choices line up. The 3D numpy stack itself is worth reconsidering: numpy=
 arrays don't grow in place, so both R and N extension force reallocation; =
you typically either keep a list-of-arrays and concat lazily, or pre-alloca=
te with doubling. For N in the thousands, packing into <code class=3D"bg-te=
xt-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre=
-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">uint64</code> rows (length=
 =E2=8C=88N/64=E2=8C=89) is ~8=C3=97 smaller than <code class=3D"bg-text-20=
0/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap=
 rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">dtype=3Dbool</code> and support=
s vectorized bitwise ops natively. And padding can stay lazy =E2=80=94 a ru=
le's bitmask is implicitly zero-extended, so memory cost stays proportional=
 to actually-set bits rather than (R =C3=97 N_max).</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The question I'd answer before going further: for a single packed-n=
ode alternative <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-borde=
r-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-=
[0.9rem]">A =E2=86=92 B C</code>, what specifically goes into bitmask-1 and=
 bitmask-2? And if <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bo=
rder-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px te=
xt-[0.9rem]">A</code> has alternatives <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border =
border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0=
.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">B C</code> and <code class=3D"bg-text-200/=
5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap r=
ounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">D E</code>, is that one rule or t=
wo =E2=80=94 and if one, how do you recover that <code class=3D"bg-text-200=
/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap =
rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">(B,C)</code> and <code class=3D"=
bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespac=
e-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">(D,E)</code> are pair=
ed and not crossed? Whatever falls out of those two answers fixes the meani=
ng of the structure and tells you whether you're representing an SPPF faith=
fully or some named lossier quotient of one.</p></div></div></div></div></d=
