=3D"sr-only" role=3D"status" aria-live=3D"polite">Enumerated packing strate=
gies and surfaced density-dependent tradeoffs</span><div class=3D"grid tran=
sition-[grid-template-rows] duration-300 ease-out" style=3D"grid-template-r=
ows: 0fr;"><div class=3D"overflow-hidden min-w-0"></div></div></div></div><=
div class=3D"row-start-2 col-start-1 relative grid isolate min-w-0"><div cl=
ass=3D"row-start-1 col-start-1 relative z-[2] min-w-0"><div><div class=3D"s=
tandard-markdown grid-cols-1 grid [&amp;_&gt;_*]:min-w-0 gap-3 standard-mar=
kdown"><p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal =
leading-[1.7]">The packing question separates cleanly into two sub-question=
s you can answer independently:</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">(a) how the bitmasks live in memory for <em>operations</em>, and
(b) how you find an existing rule with the same content for <em>dedup</em>.=
</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">These don't have to share a representation. The dense <code class=
=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 white=
space-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">(R, 2, N)</code> =
stack is great for ops =E2=80=94 vectorized AND/OR across rules, and the fr=
ee reverse lookup where column j of every rule tells you who references ite=
m j =E2=80=94 and bad for equality search (linear scan). The natural split =
is: keep the dense stack as the operational substrate, and maintain a side =
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">dict</=
code> with key =3D <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bo=
rder-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px te=
xt-[0.9rem]">stripped_packbits(left) ++ stripped_packbits(right)</code> map=
ping to index, used only for the dedup query. That side table is also where=
 the trailing-zeros equivalence gets encoded explicitly =E2=80=94 strip-the=
n-hash makes idempotent-adds idempotent across N-epochs, not just within on=
e. Otherwise the same rule submitted at N=3D10 and N=3D20 hashes differentl=
y and you've quietly broken the property you say you like.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">For the storage representation itself, the candidates as I see them=
:</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><em>Bool stack, no packing.</em> <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 borde=
r border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-=
[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">(R, 2, N)</code> as <code class=3D"bg-te=
xt-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre=
-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">bool</code>/<code class=3D=
"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespa=
ce-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">uint8</code>. One by=
te per bit. Useful for prototyping; for any realistic grammar size, the 8=
=C3=97 factor over packed bits will start to hurt.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><em>Word-packed flat.</em> <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border bord=
er-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4re=
m] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">(R, 2, W)</code> of <code class=3D"bg-text-200=
/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap =
rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">uint64</code>, <code class=3D"bg=
-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-=
pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">W =3D =E2=8C=88N/64=E2=
=8C=89</code>. Compact, bitwise ops vectorize natively, reverse lookup is s=
till cheap: <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-30=
0 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9=
rem]">(arr[:, :, j//64] &gt;&gt; (j%64)) &amp; 1</code>. The pain: growing =
N past a 64-boundary forces reallocation of the whole stack. Amortizable by=
 doubling =E2=80=94 overallocate <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border=
-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem]=
 px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">(R, 2, 2W)</code> and only fill the first W.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><em>Chunked / paged.</em> <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border borde=
r-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem=
] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">(R, 2, P, S)</code> with S a fixed page size (s=
ay 1024 bits =3D 16 uint64s) and P growing as N exceeds page boundaries. Th=
is is what I'd guess you mean by "an additional dimension." It eliminates g=
lobal reallocation on N-growth: a new page is <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 =
border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rou=
nded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">(R, 2, 1, S)</code> concatenated al=
ong axis 2, and old rules don't move. Amortized cost per chart-item-additio=
n is O(R/S), negligible. Indexing is uglier (<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 b=
order border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap roun=
ded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">arr[r, b, j//S, (j%S)//64]</code>) a=
nd cross-page bitwise ops need a loop over pages, but within a page you get=
 the same SIMD-friendly behavior as the flat version.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><em>Ragged.</em> No single 3D array; each rule keeps its own <code =
class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 =
whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">(2, W_at_bir=
th)</code>. Storage-minimal, but you give up the dense ops you said you lik=
e. Probably not what you want.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The choice between flat-packed and paged is mostly a question of ho=
w aggressively N grows during a single build. If you can pick a generous pa=
ge size and rarely cross a page boundary, flat-packed wins on simplicity. I=
f N is unbounded and you want zero reallocation hits, paged is the answer a=
nd the awkwardness of the four-axis index is the price.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The one thing the packing choice can't be fully decoupled from is b=
itmask <em>density</em>. If the common case is "one or two bits set" =E2=80=
=94 which is what binarized-SPPF (left child, right child) would give you =
=E2=80=94 then <em>any</em> dense representation wastes most of the space, =
and an index-array form (<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 bor=
der-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py=
-px text-[0.9rem]">int32[2]</code> per rule, plus a separate side-index for=
 "rules referencing item j") would dominate on every axis except elegance. =
If the common case is "many bits set" =E2=80=94 alternatives-bundled-per-sy=
mbol or similar =E2=80=94 then dense is well-matched. So the packing decisi=
on is downstream of the meaning decision, even though it doesn't feel that =
way. Worth keeping in the back of your head as you push forward.</p></div><=
