=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">=
Fair. You're right that the dense scan <em>is</em> the search =E2=80=94 <co=
de class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-0=
00 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">matches =
=3D np.all(arr[:, 0] =3D=3D B1, axis=3D1) &amp; np.all(arr[:, 1] =3D=3D B2,=
 axis=3D1)</code>, then <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 bord=
er-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-=
px text-[0.9rem]">(matches.any(), matches.argmax())</code> gives you <code =
class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 =
whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">(existed, in=
dex)</code>. For SIMD-friendly dtypes this stays cheap in absolute terms ev=
en at large R, and the architectural simplicity of "no auxiliary structure;=
 identity is a property of the array itself" is worth more than the constan=
t-factor speedup a side dict gives. I was reaching for hash-consing-by-dict=
 because that's the canonical pattern, but it's the canonical pattern for p=
ointer-graph DAGs, not numpy-backed ones.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">What it commits you to, mostly cleanly:</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The array has to be <em>present</em> at current N for the compariso=
n to be well-typed =E2=80=94 either eagerly stored that way or materialized=
 on demand by whatever sparse backend you settle on. Sparse-matrix (CSR or =
Roaring-style) is fine because the dense slice is cheap to produce. Pure ad=
jacency-list (per-rule, list of set indices) is the one storage choice that=
 fights this, because comparison becomes "two sorted-int lists for equality=
" and doesn't vectorize across R the same way. So "sparse matrix or adjacen=
cy list" isn't quite indifferent: the sparse matrix preserves the filter, t=
he adjacency list trades it away.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Bitwise equality at current N gives you trailing-zeros idempotency =
for free, because every rule is stored at current N. The old rule with impl=
icit-zero high bits and the new rule with explicit-zero high bits are liter=
ally the same row.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">One invariant worth declaring explicitly under the charter: <code c=
lass=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 w=
hitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">matches.sum()=
 =E2=89=A4 1</code> always. Two rows with identical bitmasks would mean the=
 dedup previously admitted a duplicate =E2=80=94 which it can't, by its own=
 construction. So <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bor=
der-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px tex=
t-[0.9rem]">.any()</code> and <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.=
5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px=
-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">.argmax()</code> together cover the two cases witho=
ut disambiguation needed. Worth writing down because it's the kind of thing=
 a future maintainer might add a defensive check for, not noticing that the=
 check is provably dead.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The remaining thing the design hasn't yet committed to =E2=80=94 an=
d this one really does decide the storage backend later, even if not now =
=E2=80=94 is the typical bit-density of those rows. The filter-as-search st=
ory works at any density; the choice between dense-and-keep-it-dense vs. sp=
arse-with-dense-view diverges sharply depending on whether a typical rule h=
as ~2 set bits or ~N/2. And that, in turn, falls out of whatever the two bi=
tmasks mean.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div =
