response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">Oh, that's nice.=
 The entropy argument works out: 2R ones in 2R=C2=B2 bits is density 1/R, a=
nd BWT on something that sparse with the structural regularity you've got =
=E2=80=94 every rule's masks are one-hot, and children-positions are heavil=
y reused across rules (every terminal shows up as a child of many parents) =
=E2=80=94 collapses toward the information-theoretic lower bound of ~2R log=
 R bits. Run-length BWT achieves O(r log(n/r)) where r is the number of run=
s after the transform, and for one-hot-heavy data with shared children, r w=
ill be small.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The thing I want to pin down is what role BWT plays in the stack. T=
wo readings:</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><em>Operational representation.</em> The chart lives in SM as a BWT=
'd bitstring with a wavelet-matrix or FM-index overlay. R scales much furth=
er before spilling =E2=80=94 at ~2R log R bits per chart, R can be in the t=
ens of thousands comfortably and the low hundreds of thousands at the edge =
while staying SM-resident. But every chart op becomes a rank/select query r=
ather than a bitwise op, and the "operations stay in bitmask-land" argument=
 from earlier doesn't apply =E2=80=94 you're now in rank/select-land, which=
 is a different algebra.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><em>Compressed-at-rest representation.</em> BWT is the cold-storage=
 form; you decompress to dense before operating. Small storage, fast ops, b=
ut the bandwidth cost has just moved from "load dense chart" to "load compr=
essed plus decompress." May or may not be a win depending on where decompre=
ssion sits in the pipeline and how often you reload.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">A related observation BWT brings into focus, since you mentioned th=
e same insight is what made the one-hot encoding work in the first place: w=
ith both masks one-hot, two rules are equal iff their two argmaxes match =
=E2=80=94 four integers' worth of equality, not 2R bits' worth. The dense s=
can you committed to does redundant work on the R=E2=88=921 zero bits per r=
ow. In the SM-bandwidth regime that's fine because the bits are right there=
 and the ALU work is free against the bandwidth saved. In a BWT-only world =
it's a real cost, because extracting an argmax is itself a select query, so=
 "compare two rules" goes from O(R/word) bitwise to four select calls. Wort=
h tracking which regime you're optimizing for at each step of the pipeline.=
</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class=3D"fle=
