t has rigid structure (one-hot bits at known positions, zeros everywhere el=
se), and the BWT layout is a deterministic function of content. So for each=
 new bit you can pre-compute where it lands in the post-BWT without doing t=
he full BWT =E2=80=94 the destination is determined by the bit's value and =
its rotational context, both known in advance for structured insertions. Th=
e dynamic update reduces to a small number of bit-insertions at pre-compute=
d positions plus the rank/select auxiliary updates riding along.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The "deterministic possible set of immediately-prior states" being =
a singleton vs. a larger set comes down to whether the post-state encodes t=
he insertion order. For your construction it does =E2=80=94 rule indices ar=
e assigned in insertion order, the most-recent rule is at the top of the st=
ack, and removing it plus undoing its BWT contribution leaves a unique prio=
r state. Worth checking that singleton property on a worked example before =
committing, because if it doesn't hold, the slack is where you'd discover a=
n ambiguity in the update algorithm.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div=
