-1 grid [&amp;_&gt;_*]:min-w-0 gap-3 standard-markdown"><h3 class=3D"text-t=
ext-100 mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold">Formalization</h3>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">A <strong>chart</strong> of size R is a sequence C =3D =E2=9F=A8=CF=
=81=E2=82=80, =E2=80=A6, =CF=81_{R-1}=E2=9F=A9 where each rule =CF=81=E1=B5=
=A2 =3D (L=E1=B5=A2, Q=E1=B5=A2) with L=E1=B5=A2, Q=E1=B5=A2 =E2=88=88 {0,1=
}^R, subject to one-hot-or-zero (=CE=A3=E2=B1=BC L=E1=B5=A2[j] =E2=89=A4 1 =
and same for Q=E1=B5=A2), acyclicity (set bits sit at positions strictly le=
ss than i), and hash-consing (no two rules share the same bitmask pair). Th=
e dense form is C[i, b, j] =E2=88=88 {0,1}^{R =C3=97 2 =C3=97 R}; the flat =
form is s(C) =E2=88=88 {0,1}^{2R=C2=B2} under row-major flattening; the ope=
rational form is B(C) =3D BWT(s(C)) with rank/select auxiliaries.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The <strong>structural insertion operator</strong> =CE=B6 takes a f=
lat string and a new rule and produces the flat string of the post-insertio=
n chart. It has three parts: a coordinate remap sending each old (i, b, j) =
to its new flat position under the larger R; fixed zero-padding insertions =
at the new column positions (i, b, R) for i &lt; R; and the new rule's bits=
 at the tail positions (R, b, j). The crucial property: =CE=B6 is <em>conte=
nt-blind</em> =E2=80=94 it doesn't read the bits, it permutes-and-injects b=
y position. Given R and =CF=81_R, every bit's destination is pre-computable=
.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The <strong>abstract update relation</strong> =CE=A6_R =E2=8A=86 {0=
,1}^{2R=C2=B2} =C3=97 Rules_{R+1} =C3=97 {0,1}^{2(R+1)=C2=B2} is defined by=
 =CE=A6_R(B, =CF=81, B') =E2=9F=BA =E2=88=83C. B =3D B(C) =E2=88=A7 B' =3D =
B(insert(C, =CF=81)). Viewed across all priors and posts, =CE=A6_R is many-=
to-many: "what could lead to what."</p>
<h3 class=3D"text-text-100 mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold">The witnes=
s-application principle</h3>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Concrete insertion holds a specific B (current state) and chooses a=
 specific =CF=81 (rule to commit). The post-state is the unique element of =
=CE=A6_R(B, =CF=81, =C2=B7):</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">{B'} =3D =CF=80_{B'}( =CE=A6_R =E2=88=A9 ({B} =C3=97 {=CF=81} =C3=
=97 =C2=B7 ) )</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The intersection is a singleton because BWT is bijective (B determi=
nes C), insertion is a function on (C, =CF=81) (so insert(C, =CF=81) is uni=
que), and BWT is again bijective on the result. Schematically:</p>
