</span><span>  96      18432     1264.3       2010.1</span></code></pre></d=
iv></div>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The entropy lower bound for two one-hot vectors per rule is 2R=C2=
=B7log=E2=82=82R bits. The BWT+RLE encoding runs ~60% over entropy and the =
gap widens with R. So BWT-as-compressor isn't reaching what the structure a=
ctually allows. A direct integer-position encoding =E2=80=94 store =E2=9F=
=A8left_index, right_index=E2=9F=A9 per rule as two log=E2=82=82R-bit ints =
=E2=80=94 hits the entropy bound exactly. For R=3D96 that's 1264 bits vs BW=
T-RLE's 2010 bits vs raw's 18432.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The honest reframe: BWT's value for this data isn't size reduction =
per se =E2=80=94 it's the <em>queryable compressed form</em>. An FM-index b=
uilt on the BWT supports O(log R) rank/select on the compressed bits, which=
 the integer-position encoding doesn't directly give you. If the operationa=
l picture is "many concurrent traversals on an SM-resident structure, each =
doing rank/select to walk children," BWT-as-FM-index plausibly beats intege=
r-position-plus-scan even at slightly larger total size. If the operational=
 picture is "traversal by direct integer dereferencing," the integer-positi=
on encoding wins on both axes.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Two notes on the empirical caveats. First, my synthetic chart uses =
uniformly random distinct (left, right) pairs =E2=80=94 that's roughly a wo=
rst case for BWT, because there's little shared rotational context. Real gr=
ammars with terminal-heavy bottoms (many rules pointing at the same handful=
 of leaves) would give BWT more to cluster, and the run-count ratio should =
drop further. Second, the integer-position encoding is content-blind to the=
 chart's <em>index structure</em> =E2=80=94 it just stores positions =E2=80=
=94 whereas the BWT preserves the chart-as-bitstring view that aligns with =
your "operations stay in bitmask-land" commitment from earlier in the conve=
rsation. So the question isn't "which is smaller" but "which is smaller whi=
le still letting the operations you want stay vectorizable."</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">For the witness-application principle, none of this changes the for=
malization. The pipeline <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 bor=
der-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py=
-px text-[0.9rem]">s(C=E2=82=83) =E2=86=92 =CE=B6 =E2=86=92 s(C=E2=82=84) =
=E2=86=92 BWT =E2=86=92 B(C=E2=82=84)</code> is deterministic and the scrip=
t verifies it bit-for-bit; the AND-against-witness collapse is unchanged wh=
ether the operational form is BWT or integer-positions or something else. W=
hat changes is which operational form earns the SM-residence argument from =
a few turns back. Worth deciding before committing to BWT specifically vs. =
some other compressed-queryable form.</p></div></div></div></div></div></di=
