<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">All three land within ~5% of each other, and all sit ~1.6x=E2=80=93=
1.9x over entropy. The choice is operational shape, not size.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Split-L+Q wins at small/medium R (8 through 32) by a few percent be=
cause each independent matrix gives a very clean BWT =E2=80=94 R ones surro=
unded by zeros, sorted by context, ~R+1 runs. 2bit-words wins at R=3D64 and=
 96; the alphabet-vs-length tradeoff (=CF=83=3D4 but length R=C2=B2 instead=
 of 2R=C2=B2) tips slightly in its favor as R grows.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">My original concat-1bit is strictly dominated by split-L+Q at every=
 size tested. The reason makes sense in retrospect: row-major flattening pu=
ts each rule's L row adjacent to its unrelated Q row, which manufactures cr=
oss-context rotations that don't reflect any real structure in the data. Sp=
litting them lets each BWT handle its own coherent one-hot matrix. So the r=
ow-major default was a bad call; if you stay with BWT at all, the split is =
the right starting layout.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">None of the three approaches the entropy bound, which integer-posit=
ion encoding hits exactly. So whatever value BWT carries here is not raw-si=
ze minimization =E2=80=94 it's the queryable compressed form (rank/select v=
ia FM-index or wavelet tree). If the operational picture is two independent=
 traversal flows (which seems right for one-hot L vs. one-hot Q), split-L+Q=
 gives you that natively, and the alphabet stays binary so the rank/select =
machinery is the simplest variant. 2bit-words is marginally smaller at scal=
e but requires a wavelet tree over a 4-symbol alphabet, which is more machi=
nery for a few percent of bits.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Worth checking the Zipfian in-degree case before committing =E2=80=
=94 that's where my earlier hand-waving was, and the answer might shift the=
 verdict between split-L+Q and 2bit-words, though I'd be surprised if any o=
f the three suddenly closed the gap to entropy.</p></div></div></div></div>=
