<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The more important finding: the ~1.6=E2=80=931.9x gap to the unifor=
m-entropy bound persists across skew. It's not that the entropy bound itsel=
f is invariant (skewed distributions have lower actual entropy than 2R=C2=
=B7log=E2=82=82R), but the BWT-based encoding's ratio to <em>whatever</em> =
the entropy is doesn't collapse as the distribution gets more realistic. Th=
e structural overhead of BWT+RLE on this data stays roughly constant in pro=
portional terms.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">So the "absurdly compact" framing from a few turns back doesn't bea=
r out empirically =E2=80=94 at least not via BWT, and not at any of the in-=
degree distributions I tested. BWT compresses by ~2x vs. raw (2R=C2=B2 bits=
