<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Some patterns worth noting:</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The 2bit-words scheme is where canonical ordering pays off most. Be=
st case is R=3D96 / s=3D2 / indeg-desc / 2bit-words: 2121 bits vs insertion=
's 2229, ~5% improvement. R=3D32 / s=3D2 / indeg-desc / 2bit-words gets a b=
igger relative gain (512 vs 578, ~11%), but at toy R the variance is noisy.=
</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The mechanism: in-degree-descending puts heavily-referenced rules a=
t low positions, so the bits "j=3D0, j=3D1, j=3D2, =E2=80=A6" in every rule=
's bitmasks are the bits most often set. The 2-bit alphabet at each (rule, =
position) cell co-locates L and Q, so popular positions accumulate same-sym=
bol clusters that BWT exploits. Merkle ordering doesn't help as much becaus=
e hash-consed rules are all structurally distinct, so the Merkle hash sort =
is essentially random within each depth band =E2=80=94 there's no structure=
-similarity to cluster.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">For the other two layout schemes, canonical ordering is roughly a w=
ash and sometimes <em>hurts</em> (concat-1bit gets worse under Merkle in se=
veral rows). Splitting L and Q kills the cross-bitmask correlation that the=
 2-bit alphabet preserves, so the benefit of putting popular targets at low=
 positions doesn't survive the split.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The compression gain is real but modest (~5% in the best realistic =
case). The bigger argument for canonical ordering is the one your note alre=
ady named: determinism across loads. Two grammars with identical structure =
produce identical charts, which makes caching, comparison, and content-addr=
essing free. That's worth more than the 5% in most settings =E2=80=94 but i=
t costs you the "new rule is at the top of the stack" property that made th=
e insertion + AND-against-witness story so clean a few turns back. You'd ne=
ed a canonical-ordering-aware insert, which is more bookkeeping.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">So: canonical ordering buys determinism + a slim compression win, c=
osts the insertion-order simplicity. Whether the trade is worth it depends =
on whether cross-load identity matters more than incremental-construction s=
implicity for the use case.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></=
