<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">I want to make sure I'm reading the proposal right before running a=
nything, because "heap-sort with topological comparator" parses two ways wi=
th very different consequences:</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">(a) heap-sort is just the sorting algorithm; the output is a canoni=
cal linear array in topological order, and "relative addressing" means bitm=
asks encode deltas-from-self instead of absolute positions. This is a permu=
tation plus a reinterpretation of bitmask semantics =E2=80=94 the structure=
 stays a flat array, only the encoding shifts.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">(b) the heap layout <em>is</em> the addressing scheme =E2=80=94 nod=
e at array index <em>i</em> has its heap-children at 2i+1 and 2i+2, parent =
at (i-1)/2. The chart's DAG edges get encoded as paths through this implici=
t binary tree rather than as direct pointers. The ID=E2=86=94node maps make=
 sense here because the heap has its own coordinate space distinct from any=
 external identity, and "walk tree" is the lookup primitive.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Reading (b) is the more radical one =E2=80=94 it changes the addres=
sing primitive from "bit set at absolute position" to "path through the imp=
licit binary tree." Variable-length addresses, but related nodes share path=
 prefixes, so delta encoding gets cheap.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Which reading is closer to what you're imagining? Or is there a thi=
