<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The bit count is consistently worse than direct integer encoding =
=E2=80=94 by 55% at small R and uniform distribution, dropping to ~8% at R=
=3D96 with skew. The gap closes but never crosses.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The reason it loses on raw bits: heap-paths are still O(log R) leng=
th on average (LCA is typically near the root for random pairs), and the va=
riable-length encoding adds prefix overhead (up_count + down_length headers=
) that direct fixed-width integers don't pay. With Zipfian skew the mean pa=
th length drops because many edges go to popular near-root targets, but the=
 prefix overhead is paid per edge regardless.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">So heap-relative addressing isn't an <em>encoding</em> win. What it=
 could still be worth:</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The heap-walk primitives are pure integer bitops =E2=80=94 <code cl=
ass=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 wh=
itespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">parent =3D (i-=
1) &gt;&gt; 1</code>, <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border=
-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px=
 text-[0.9rem]">left =3D (i &lt;&lt; 1) + 1</code>, <code class=3D"bg-text-=
200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wr=
ap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">right =3D (i &lt;&lt; 1) + 2<=
/code> =E2=80=94 so traversal stays in the "operations are cheap on a resid=
ent structure" regime you've been valuing. The layout is canonical given th=
e input (modulo same-depth tiebreakers), so two loads of the same grammar p=
roduce identical layouts. And heap-level locality (same-depth nodes within =
a stride) gives some cache-line coherence for traversal that bitmask-scatte=
red layouts don't.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">None of those are compression arguments though. If size is the metr=
ic, the integer-encoding-with-canonical-permutation result from a couple tu=
rns back is still the best I've measured. The heap-relative scheme buys you=
 canonical layout + bitwise-traversal-walks at the cost of ~10-50% encoding=
 overhead. Worth it depending on what the operational picture looks like; n=
ot worth it if you're optimizing bytes.</p></div></div></div></div></div></=
