rid [&amp;_&gt;_*]:min-w-0 gap-3 standard-markdown"><p class=3D"font-claude=
-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">Yeah, exactly. =
Heap positions are a 1D Morton encoding of a binary tree =E2=80=94 the bina=
ry representation of position i (1-indexed) is the path from the root, wher=
e each bit after the leading 1 is a left/right choice. Siblings share all b=
ut their last bit; deeper relatives share longer prefixes; the LCA depth of=
 two positions is the number of leading bits they have in common.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Several things follow:</p>
<ul class=3D"[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:l=
ast-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-co=
l gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
The path from i to j is encoded by <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border bord=
er-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4re=
m] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">i XOR j</code>. The MSB of the XOR marks the L=
CA depth; the bits below it are the path from LCA down to j (taking j's low=
er bits directly).</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
LCA-of-two-positions is <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 bord=
er-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-=
px text-[0.9rem]">clz(i XOR j)</code> =E2=80=94 a single leading-zeros-coun=
t instruction.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Parent / left-child / right-child are arithmetic shifts; "is i a descendant=
 of j" is a bit-prefix comparison.</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">So heap-walking <em>is</em> bit-twiddling on integer positions, in =
exactly the regime you wanted to stay in. "ID =E2=86=92 node, walk tree, no=
de =E2=86=92 ID" becomes: external ID =E2=86=92 heap position, traverse via=
 XOR/CLZ/shift on the integer, heap position =E2=86=92 external ID. No bitm=
ask matrices needed for the addressing layer at all.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">What the Morton framing doesn't change: the entropy. Encoding a tar=
get as <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 tex=
t-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]"=
>(source XOR target)</code> instead of <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border =
border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0=
.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">target</code> is the same bit count for ra=
ndom pairs, because XOR is a bijection. Where it helps is if many edges hav=
e <em>similar</em> <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bo=
rder-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px te=
xt-[0.9rem]">source XOR target</code> values =E2=80=94 same offset pattern =
repeating across the chart. For the random and Zipfian synthetics we tested=
, that pattern didn't show up much, but real grammars with structural regul=
arity (the same production schema reused with children at predictable relat=
ive heap-offsets) might give XOR-clusters that compress further. That'd be =
the empirical question worth running on actual grammar data rather than syn=
thetic.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class=
