s-1 grid [&amp;_&gt;_*]:min-w-0 gap-3 standard-markdown"><p class=3D"font-c=
laude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">Right =E2=
=80=94 that's why all the schemes have been circling the same total bit cou=
nt. A one-hot mask of length R, an integer in [0, R), and a path of log=E2=
=82=82R left/right moves through a binary tree with R leaves are isomorphic=
 representations of "one element from a set of R." Only the operational lay=
er changes between them:</p>
<ul class=3D"[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:l=
ast-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-co=
l gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
one-hot: R bits, mask AND/OR/XOR</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
integer: log=E2=82=82R bits, shifts/XOR/CLZ</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
path: log=E2=82=82R bits as a sequence, same ops as integer (since the bits=
 <em>are</em> the path)</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The last equivalence is the one that also collapses "absolute integ=
er encoding" and "heap-relative path encoding" =E2=80=94 once you read the =
integer's bits as the path through the encoding tree, they're the same bits=
 read differently. <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bo=
rder-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px te=
xt-[0.9rem]">i XOR j</code> is the divergence pattern; leading bits in comm=
on form the prefix to the LCA in the encoding tree; trailing bits are the s=
uffix from LCA to target.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">What this might also collapse: the "store the one-hot mask or store=
 the integer" question. Both describe the same object; only the operations =
differ. The earlier SM-residence argument for one-hot was about a specific =
operational tradeoff =E2=80=94 does every step need to vectorize against ex=
isting mask state in parallel, or is per-step bit-twiddling on integers acc=
eptable? Integer-as-path stays SM-resident at much larger R (log=E2=82=82R =
vs R bits per pointer), keeps operations bitwise (just bit-twiddling rather=
 than bitmask-vectorized), and gives you heap-walking by the same bits.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Worth being explicit about which operational property you actually =
want to keep =E2=80=94 there's a real tradeoff but it's not the one we'd be=
en treating it as.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div=
