in-w-0 gap-3 standard-markdown"><p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break=
-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">Right =E2=80=94 that maps cleanly o=
nto SIMT. Each thread holds one current node in a register, all threads in =
a warp execute the same instruction ("load my node's left child index"), an=
d the parallelism comes from many warps =C3=97 many lanes =C3=97 many SMs, =
all doing the same per-step bit-twiddling on different starting frontiers. =
The chart is shared (read-only during traversal); the frontier state is per=
-thread.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The frontier-set bitmask matvec was actually doing the same total w=
ork =E2=80=94 N bit-ops to advance N positions =E2=80=94 just packed into o=
ne wide SIMD operation. SIMT unpacks that into N narrow ops across hardware=
 lanes. On a GPU that's the more native partitioning: one integer per lane,=
 no waste on zero positions, low register pressure, shared-memory reads spr=
ead across banks.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The one thing to watch is bank conflicts on the children-pointer re=
ads =E2=80=94 different lanes hitting different chart positions is fine, bu=
t if the access pattern happens to bunch at the same shared-memory bank in =
a cycle, the warp serializes. With consecutive rule indices laid out across=
 consecutive banks (the default for any sane stride), you should be at peak=
 throughput. The harder case is if many threads converge on the same popula=
r target node (e.g., a common terminal at heap position 0); that's broadcas=
t-load territory, which modern SMs handle as a single shared read rather th=
an a conflict, but worth knowing it's the access pattern most likely to hit=
 the fast path on hot targets.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div=
