0"><div><div class=3D"standard-markdown grid-cols-1 grid [&amp;_&gt;_*]:min=
-w-0 gap-3 standard-markdown"><p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-w=
ords whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">Bank conflicts are within a single SM=
 =E2=80=94 specifically within a single warp's instruction issue, against t=
hat SM's shared memory. Different SMs have entirely separate shared memory =
pools and don't interfere on shared memory access. If your chart lives in s=
hared memory loaded per-SM (the standard pattern when it fits), each SM is =
already operating on its own copy automatically =E2=80=94 "replicate" is wh=
at's happening by default; you load from global memory into each SM's share=
d memory once at kernel start.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">NVIDIA shared memory is 32 banks of 4-byte words, one access per ba=
nk per cycle. When 32 lanes issue loads in the same cycle: if they hit 32 d=
ifferent banks, full throughput in one cycle; if multiple lanes hit differe=
nt addresses in the same bank, the access serializes by the conflict count;=
 if multiple lanes hit the same address in the same bank, the SM detects it=
 and broadcasts in one cycle. So the same-address case isn't a conflict at =
all =E2=80=94 that's exactly the case where every thread converges on the p=
opular terminal at position 0.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">If the chart doesn't fit in shared memory and lives in L2 or global=
 memory, the cross-SM concern is different: L2 bandwidth is shared across a=
ll SMs and is much lower than per-SM shared memory throughput. L2 handles h=
ot-line broadcasts efficiently too, but total bandwidth is the limit. "Fits=
 in shared memory per-SM" is the regime where the architecture really sings=
 =E2=80=94 once you've crossed into needing L2 or global, you're back into =
bandwidth-bound territory, just with a higher ceiling than naive global mem=
ory.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class=3D=
