arkdown"><p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-norma=
l leading-[1.7]">Right, I dismissed too quickly. Over GF(2), the C-D operat=
ions simplify and become exactly the bitwise ops we're already using:</p>
<ul class=3D"[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:l=
ast-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-co=
l gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Addition</strong> is XOR =E2=80=94 already in use for path divergen=
ce.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Conjugation</strong> is identity at every level (induction: base ca=
se is <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text=
-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">=
a =3D a</code> over GF(2); inductive step is <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 b=
order border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap roun=
ded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">conj((a,b)) =3D (conj(a), -b) =3D (a=
, b)</code> since <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bor=
der-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px tex=
t-[0.9rem]">-b =3D b</code> in characteristic 2). Free.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Multiplication</strong> via the C-D recursion (a,b)(c,d) =3D (ac - =
d=CC=84b, da + bc=CC=84) collapses since both subtraction and conjugation v=
anish: (a,b)(c,d) =3D (ac + db, da + bc). A specific bit-pattern combiner, =
recursively defined by the same doubling tree as the fat node.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Norm</strong> N(z) =3D z=C2=B7conj(z) reduces to the XOR-fold over =
all 2^k components =E2=80=94 the parity bit. So the "norm" of a fat node is=
 the parity of its presence pattern. One XOR-tree to compute, log=E2=82=82(=
2^k) =3D k depth.</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The multiplication itself has a nice interpretation at level 1: (a,=
b)(c,d) =3D (ac+bd, ad+bc). The first component is the bitwise dot-product =
of the two pairs =E2=80=94 "how much do these two halves overlap as bit pat=
terns." The second is the "cross-term." At higher levels these recurse: a f=
at-node multiply gives you a layered overlap-and-cross-term decomposition, =
recursively, in O(k log k) GF(2) ops on the descriptor registers.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">So the descriptor's algebra isn't just structural =E2=80=94 it's a =
hardware-native ring with operations that have plausible chart-meaningful i=
nterpretations: parity (whole-subtree XOR fingerprint, useful as a cheap ha=
sh), overlap (dot-product-style), and recursive structural combination. All=
 while staying inside the same SIMD register width that motivated picking k=
=3D4 in the first place.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">What specifically did you have in mind for the algebraic ops =E2=80=
=94 fingerprinting, subtree-equality probes, something operating on descrip=
tor pairs?</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div cl=
