Edge fetches come back already in the form the next stage wants.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The general pattern: anything that's a function of a fixed point in=
 the chart can be computed once at insertion and stored, because the chart =
never rewrites earlier entries. Acyclicity plus monotonic growth makes this=
 much cleaner than in a mutable structure =E2=80=94 precomputed values are =
valid forever, never invalidated. So the cost calculus changes: an "expensi=
ve" operation done once at insert and read many times during traversal effe=
ctively becomes a free constant in the steady-state hot path. The insertion=
 path gets more work; the traversal path gets cleaner data.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">It also generalizes: you can keep <em>multiple</em> precomputed vie=
ws of the chart side by side =E2=80=94 raw IDs for one operation, fingerpri=
nts for another, AES-mixed descriptors for a third =E2=80=94 and pick the v=
iew that matches the query. The storage cost is linear in views per rule, w=
hich trades cleanly against SM-residence at moderate N.</p></div></div></di=
