0 gap-3 standard-markdown"><p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-word=
s whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">The cleanest way to walk through this is=
 by use case rather than by technique =E2=80=94 for each operation the char=
t supports, which precomputation pays off and which doesn't.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Hash-consing during construction.</strong> The dedup check =
is local: given new children (l, q), find any existing rule with that same =
pair. Linear scan on (l, q) integer pairs, vectorized. None of the three pr=
ecomputations directly accelerate this =E2=80=94 hash-consing already gives=
 perfect structural dedup at the (l, q) granularity, so subtree fingerprint=
s would be redundant for the check itself. Fingerprints buy you something a=
t insert time only if you want to also answer "did this subtree exist befor=
e via a different path," which is a different question than hash-consing an=
swers.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Single-step traversal.</strong> Load rule k's (l, q), pick =
one, move on. Dominant cost is load latency; precomputation helps when a un=
iform transformation is applied to loaded data on every step =E2=80=94 desc=
riptor unpacking from bit-packed storage, or an S-box mix. Pre-mixed descri=
ptors fold the transform into storage; LUT-applied edges fold an ID transfo=
rm into storage. Saves 1-3 cycles per step. Worth doing if ALU is starting =
to bind, neutral otherwise.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Multi-step fat-node traversal.</strong> Each fat-node load =
is followed by k path-extraction selects. If path-extraction involves a fix=
ed per-level mix (XOR against a mask, S-box per level), pre-mixing the desc=
riptor folds those k mix ops into storage. The fat-node descriptor was alre=
ady a precomputation (depth-k subtree summary); pre-mixing is the second la=
yer on top of that.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Subtree-equality probes.</strong> "Is subtree(A) =E2=89=A1 =
subtree(B)?" Without precomputation: walk both, compare, O(subtree size). W=
ith per-rule fingerprints: compare scalars, O(1). This is where fingerprint=
s actually shine =E2=80=94 they collapse a structural query into a scalar c=
ompare. Useful for cross-cutting equality checks that hash-consing doesn't =
catch (e.g., comparing subtrees across non-adjacent regions or across chart=
s).</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Subtree-keyed memoization.</strong> Downstream processing (=
typechecking, evaluation, transformation) that wants to cache results by st=
ructural identity uses the fingerprint as the cache key. Precomputed finger=
prints make both the lookup and the key small =E2=80=94 a single field elem=
ent instead of a structural reference.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Cross-chart operations.</strong> Merging two charts (differ=
ent GPUs, or chart-of-chart for a federated parser) needs to identify commo=
n subtrees so they get shared after merge. Fingerprints make this scan line=
ar in the smaller chart rather than quadratic.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The unifying observation: precomputation pays off where the same ex=
pensive operation happens on the hot path many times for the same data. The=
 chart's acyclicity makes precomputed values valid forever, so the only cos=
t is one-time work at insert. Whether a given precomputation earns its stor=
age depends on which queries you run frequently =E2=80=94 fingerprints earn=
 it for equality-heavy workloads, pre-mixed descriptors and LUT-applied edg=
es earn it for traversal-heavy workloads with uniform transformations. Neit=
her earns it for hash-consing alone, which is already optimal at the (l, q)=
 integer granularity.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></=
