ard-markdown"><p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-=
normal leading-[1.7]">Right =E2=80=94 the broader frame is: the bits we're =
storing can carry more information than just "two child IDs," and the extra=
ction cost can be small enough to fit in the spare cycles we'd otherwise bu=
rn on load-wait.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Specific patterns this opens up for the chart:</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Position-encoded metadata.</strong> With heap-relative layo=
ut, the ID itself already encodes depth (via leading-bit position) and with=
in-level position (via lower bits). Free metadata: every traversal step lea=
rns the depth of the next node without storing it separately. Generalizes: =
reserve some high bits of the ID for explicit metadata =E2=80=94 "is this a=
 terminal," "category code," "subtree size class" =E2=80=94 and pay a few c=
ycles of mask-and-shift at query time. The chart isn't 32-bit-ID-bound, it'=
s log=E2=82=82N-bit-ID-bound, so the high bits of a word are free real esta=
te.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Reachability / position labels.</strong> Each rule gets a s=
mall label computed at insert time. For a pure tree this can be an interval=
 [lo, hi] such that A is a descendant of B iff A's interval sits inside B's=
. For a DAG with sharing (which our chart is), labels need to be richer =E2=
=80=94 2-hop labels, chain decompositions =E2=80=94 but still much smaller =
than the subtrees they let you query. A few cycles to combine labels, versu=
s O(subtree size) for naive walking.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Succinct tree encoding.</strong> The tree structure itself =
(which rules are children of which) can be stored in 2N + o(N) bits using L=
OUDS or balanced parentheses, with rank/select supporting parent/child quer=
ies in a few cycles. A parallel succinct skeleton alongside the ID-valued c=
hart. Useful if you want to walk by structure ("the third child at depth 4"=
) rather than by ID.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Reference / delta encoding.</strong> When many rules have s=
imilar children patterns, encode each rule's children as a delta from a cho=
sen reference rule's children. Decompression is one XOR with the reference =
data plus a small fixup. Hash-consing already absorbs most local redundancy=
, but for grammars with highly regular structure there can still be a 2-3=
=C3=97 storage win.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Algebraic identifiers.</strong> If the ID itself is the pol=
ynomial representation of a path in GF(2^k), some arithmetic ops have direc=
t structural meaning: XOR gives the divergence pattern (LCA info), shifts g=
ive parent/child relations. Other ops (GF multiplication, S-box mixing) giv=
e fingerprints or transformed encodings rather than direct structural queri=
es. The algebra gives you a few cheap structural primitives plus a family o=
f fingerprinting operations, all sharing the same bits.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The unifying move: stop treating the bits as "just a child ID" and =
start treating them as a small program for recovering whatever structural f=
act you care about, with recovery cost fitting in the cycles we already hav=
e. The load is the expensive thing; everything done on the loaded value is =
essentially free up to a point =E2=80=94 and that point is much further awa=
y than the bare 2log=E2=82=82N bits of "two child pointers" implies.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Worth thinking about which structural queries actually matter on yo=
ur hot path. Depth, subtree-size, terminal-vs-internal classification, and =
ancestor-of probes are common; choosing two or three to encode densely and =
letting the rest go to slow paths is usually how this pays off in practice.=
</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class=3D"fle=
