id-cols-1 grid [&amp;_&gt;_*]:min-w-0 gap-3 standard-markdown"><p class=3D"=
font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">That=
 reframes the discipline. The design problem becomes: identify the chart's =
universal structure (the freest object compatible with the domain constrain=
ts =E2=80=94 acyclic, hash-consed, binary, monotonically-growing), then mai=
ntain encodings such that each desired projection from that universal struc=
ture has an efficient dual.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The freest codomain compatible with those constraints is the initia=
l term algebra generated by <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 =
border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1=
 py-px text-[0.9rem]">Rule(l, q) =E2=86=92 Index</code> modulo hash-consing=
. Every chart IS such a term algebra; every operation we've discussed =E2=
=80=94 fingerprinting, traversal, dedup, subtree-equality, reachability =E2=
=80=94 is a homomorphism out of it. The encodings we've enumerated (one-hot=
, integer, Morton/heap-relative, fat nodes, GF(2^k), succinct trees, precom=
puted fingerprints) are different concrete duals for different projection s=
ubsets. None is "the right" encoding absolutely; each is the right encoding=
 for some subset of the codomain.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">So the framework's structure: store the universal core (the (l, q) =
pair per rule, indexed by insertion order =E2=80=94 this is the term algebr=
a in its most compact form), and maintain auxiliary views, each justified b=
y a projection. The core is invariant; the auxiliaries are choices.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The design's open question then reduces to: characterize the codoma=
in. What projections are we choosing among? From the conversation so far:</=
p>
<ul class=3D"[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:l=
ast-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-co=
l gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Forward traversal =E2=80=94 load child IDs given parent.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Hash-consing =E2=80=94 equality of (l, q) pairs at insert.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Subtree-equality probes =E2=80=94 structural identity of derivations.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Reachability / ancestor queries =E2=80=94 "is A in subtree(B)?"</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Fingerprinting / structural identity.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Position metadata =E2=80=94 depth, subtree size, category.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Algebraic queries over GF(2^k) =E2=80=94 LCA, path-divergence, polynomial f=
ingerprints.</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Each is a projection from the codomain of "everything you could ask=
 of the chart." The framework's job is to support arbitrary subsets by comp=
osing auxiliary views, without committing to a single hot path. Cost-vs-ben=
efit for each auxiliary view becomes a local question about which projectio=
ns are useful enough to justify their storage.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The categorical move here is the right one =E2=80=94 picking the un=
iversal structure first, then duals, makes the design decomposable rather t=
han monolithic. Each projection's encoding is a localized choice that doesn=
't constrain the others. The framework becomes a library of duals over a sh=
ared core.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">One question worth asking next: does the codomain itself have algeb=
raic structure that the encoding should preserve? If the codomain is just a=
 discrete collection of distinct projections, each gets its own dual indepe=
ndently. But if the codomain has its own composition structure =E2=80=94 e.=
g., projections that compose (like "fingerprint of subtree" =E2=88=98 "extr=
act subtree at k"), or projections related by quotient (like "fingerprint" =
being a coarsening of "full subtree") =E2=80=94 then we'd want the encoding=
 to respect that structure, not just enumerate the projections separately. =
The GF(2^k) angle is suggestive here: algebraic identifiers preserve compos=
ition natively, so the encoding-respects-structure property comes for free.=
 Whether the full codomain forms a meaningful algebraic object =E2=80=94 a =
monoid, a lattice, a category in its own right =E2=80=94 changes how the fr=
amework should be organized internally.</p></div></div></div></div></div></=
