0 gap-3 standard-markdown"><p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-word=
s whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">That recontextualizes everything. The co=
nstraints aren't a design choice =E2=80=94 they're forced by the semantics =
of self-extending self-describing grammars, and the SPPF is the natural car=
rier because it dual-purposes parse-tree nodes as grammar rules.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Each constraint maps to a semantic requirement:</p>
<ul class=3D"[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:l=
ast-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-co=
l gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Hash-consing</strong> is the canonicalization that makes "the same =
rule" mean what it should. Two different parses producing structurally iden=
tical rules MUST be the same rule, or the grammar isn't well-defined.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Acyclicity (rule k references only rules &lt; k)</strong> is well-f=
oundedness of grammar extension: new rules are defined in terms of existing=
 ones, never themselves or future ones. This is what makes the grammar boot=
strappable.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Binary</strong> is the term-algebra normal form. Higher-arity produ=
ctions decompose to nested binary pairs. The chart IS the term algebra.</li=
>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Monotonic growth</strong> is grammar-extension semantics: rules add=
ed, never retracted. Parser state stays monotone.</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The deeper point is the SPPF dual-purposing nodes: parsing grammar =
text produces SPPF nodes which ARE grammar rules. Hash-consing collapses th=
e two roles automatically =E2=80=94 a parsed rule and the rule that parses =
such things coincide when their structures match. That's the metacircular f=
ixpoint at the storage level.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">This retroactively justifies several design choices we made:</p>
<ul class=3D"[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:l=
ast-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-co=
l gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Integer-as-path encoding gives every rule a canonical structural address. F=
or a content-addressed grammar, the encoding IS the reference scheme.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Subtree fingerprinting earns its keep: when you parse new grammar text, the=
 resulting rule's fingerprint tells you in O(1) whether you've already got =
that exact rule.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
The Morton/heap-relative layout means new rules at the construction frontie=
r have predictable locations relative to the rules they reference.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Fat-node depth-k descriptors let you "look k levels into a rule's structure=
" =E2=80=94 useful when the parser needs to recognize a partial production =
before committing to it.</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The codomain projections become specific to grammar self-interpreta=
tion: "is this rule a meta-rule," "does this rule match schema X," "is rule=
 A in the transitive closure of rule B's references," "what fixpoint does t=
his grammar definition reach." Those are the operations to make cheap.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The framework-via-universal-structure approach is especially apt fo=
r this domain: a self-extending grammar doesn't know in advance what querie=
s it will be asked, because new rules can introduce new operations. Designi=
ng around the universal core means new projections can be added later as ne=
w query types emerge, without disturbing existing storage. The chart is a l=
ibrary that grows its own clients.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">One worth thinking about explicitly: self-describing grammars usual=
ly have stratification =E2=80=94 meta-grammar, grammar, target language. Wh=
ether your design expresses the stratification structurally (a metadata bit=
 marking level, distinct chart regions per level, etc.) or relies on the ru=
les themselves to enforce it is a real choice. Structural stratification ma=
kes meta-queries cheap but bakes in an assumption about how many levels exi=
st; reflective stratification is fully general but pushes stratification lo=
gic into the rules. The realizability charter you opened with would lean to=
ward "make the distinction constructible if you want to query it" =E2=80=94=
 so if meta-vs-object level distinctions are going to be load-bearing for a=
ny projection, encode them; if they're not, leave them implicit and let the=
 rules handle it.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div>=
