div class=3D"standard-markdown grid-cols-1 grid [&amp;_&gt;_*]:min-w-0 gap-=
3 standard-markdown"><p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whit=
espace-normal leading-[1.7]">A compiler doesn't compute Ackermann(4, 2). It=
 transforms the rules <em>describing</em> Ackermann into rules easier to ex=
ecute, but never tries to reach the value at compile time. The transformati=
ons themselves always terminate; the value, when actually requested at runt=
ime, might take longer than the heat death of the universe to produce.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">What does this tell us? We've been conflating two different things:=
</p>
<ul class=3D"[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:l=
ast-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-co=
l gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Apply-as-step</strong>: take a rule and a value, perform one struct=
ural rewrite, produce the next rule. Always terminates because it's just pa=
ttern match + substitution.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Apply-as-normalize</strong>: repeatedly apply-as-step until no furt=
her rewrites are possible. May not terminate.</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">A compiler operates almost entirely in apply-as-step territory: inl=
ine this call, fold this constant, fuse these operations, hash-cons these s=
ubexpressions, specialize on known arguments, memoize the call graph. Each =
transformation is a bounded structural move. The compiler doesn't try to no=
rmalize Ackermann; it just produces a transformed program that, when invoke=
d, will normalize Ackermann.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Founding apply is single-step.</strong> Normalization-to-fi=
xed-point is a <em>derived</em> operation, expressible in the grammar as <c=
ode class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-=
000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">normaliz=
e(k) =3D if eq(apply(k), k) then k else normalize(apply(k))</code>. Its ter=
mination is a property of the specific program being normalized, not of the=
 system.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">This resolves the M3 residual tension cleanly, with no need for <co=
de class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-0=
00 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">apply_fue=
led</code>:</p>
<ul class=3D"[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:l=
ast-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-co=
l gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
The founding <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-3=
00 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.=
9rem]">apply</code> is always-terminating. Charter passes cleanly: construc=
tible (pattern-match + substitute is one bounded operation), reachable (eve=
ry redex can be reduced), observable (the result of a single step is a defi=
nite rule), coverable (the step's output set is bounded by the rule's size)=
.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Normalization is derived; its non-termination is a property of <em>programs=
 that build on apply</em>, not of the substrate. Some programs have termina=
ting normalizations; some don't. The system itself only performs finite, we=
ll-defined steps.</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">This unifies compile time and run time at the founding layer. The c=
ompiler's transformations (inlining, constant folding, partial evaluation, =
supercompilation, all of Futamura's projections) are apply-as-step. The run=
time interpreter's reductions are also apply-as-step. The distinction betwe=
en compile time and run time becomes a <em>scheduling concern</em> =E2=80=
=94 how many steps to perform before stopping =E2=80=94 not a primitive dis=
tinction.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">For Ackermann: a compiler can do considerable apply-as-step work in=
 advance =E2=80=94 recognize A(0, n) =3D n+1, A(1, n) =3D n+2, A(2, n) =3D =
2n+3 patterns, specialize for known m values, memoize the call graph, build=
 closed-form derivatives where they exist. All these terminate because they=
're structural. The compiled code, when run with A(4, 2), performs actual r=
eductions step by step; each step terminates, but the sequence may be astro=
nomical. The compiler did what it could; the runtime does the rest.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Let me update the cotype:</p></div></div></div></div></div></div><d=
