y</code> step produces a rule that hash-consing canonicalizes, so the <em>o=
utput</em> of any computation is automatically shared with any other comput=
ation producing the same structure. The chart accumulates results.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">What this means:</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Optimization is automatic.</strong> Compile-time transforma=
tions and run-time reductions both write to the same chart; both benefit fr=
om structures already present. When the system computes A(2, n) =3D 2n+3 fo=
r some n, that closed-form derivation lives in the chart as rules. Subseque=
nt computations hit those rules directly. The "compiler optimization" of re=
cognizing A(0, n) =3D n+1 isn't a separate analysis pass =E2=80=94 it's a r=
ule the system can construct, store, and apply.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>The system improves with use.</strong> Each computation add=
s rules to the monotone chart. The first time A(3, 5) is computed, the syst=
em does the full reduction; subsequent times, the result-rule and the relev=
ant intermediate rules are already there. Accumulated work persists =E2=80=
=94 the chart never invalidates.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Compile-time and run-time blur.</strong> A compiler is "the=
 system computing apply steps before the user requests them." A runtime is =
"computing apply steps when the user requests them." Same chart, same write=
s, same reads. The distinction is <em>when</em>, not <em>what</em>.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]"><strong>Closed-form rules are first-class chart entries.</strong> W=
hen the system recognizes A(0, n) =E2=86=92 n+1, that's a rule like any oth=
er: a rewrite from one pattern to another. Once constructed, it lives in th=
e chart and is automatically consulted by <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 bord=
er border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded=
-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">apply</code> on matching patterns. The =
compiler's "smart optimization" is a learned rule, accumulating like everyt=
hing else.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">A subtle distinction worth being explicit about: hash-consing gives=
 <strong>result memoization</strong> automatically (constructed rules are c=
anonical). For full <strong>computation memoization</strong> =E2=80=94 so t=
hat apply(k) is O(1) on second invocation =E2=80=94 we additionally want a =
memo table recording (input k =E2=86=92 output apply(k)) pairs. That table =
is itself rules in the chart: a "memo entry" rule with input and output as =
children. Apply's first step becomes: look up a memo entry for k; if found,=
 return the cached output; if not, compute and store.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The memo table is hash-consed like everything else. The system gets=
 meta-memoization for free =E2=80=94 memoizing the memo table, memoizing th=
e lookups in it, all in the same chart.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">For Ackermann: a system that has computed A(0, 1), A(0, 2), ..., A(=
0, n) has those results as memo entries. A new request for A(0, k) where k =
=E2=89=A4 n hits the memo directly. The system can also construct the close=
d-form rule "A(0, _) =E2=86=92 _+1" once, and from then on, apply uses that=
 single rule instead of consulting per-instance memo entries. The "compiler=
 optimization" is itself a learned rule that ascends from observed cases to=
 a general pattern.</p>
