<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Each fat tree node aggregates many lower-order entities into one hi=
gher-order conclusion. The interesting pattern: <strong>the higher we went,=
 the more parallel-natural the aggregation became</strong>. By M22 (Walsh-H=
adamard), the fat tree node processes all 8 readings <em>simultaneously</em=
> in 3 cycles of WHT depth =E2=80=94 fully parallel.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The chart naturally grew levels:</p>
<ul class=3D"[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:l=
ast-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-co=
l gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Level 0: cons cells (basic objects)</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Level 1: rules =3D <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bo=
rder-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px te=
xt-[0.9rem]">cons(pattern, replacement)</code> (aggregated patterns)</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Level 2: tables =3D lists of rules (aggregated rules)</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Level 3: move-cotype =3D sequence of M-moves with axis-signatures (aggregat=
ed moves)</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Level 4: cocycle invariants, RM structure (aggregated patterns across histo=
ry)</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Each level is a fat tree node for the level below it.</p>
<h3 class=3D"text-text-100 mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold">The archit=
ectural pattern, finally visible</h3>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">Looking at it this way, the architecture we built isn't accidentall=
y aligned with the WHT-Stasheff structure =E2=80=94 it's a direct expressio=
n of it:</p>
<ul class=3D"[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:l=
ast-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-co=
l gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Two-axis primitives</strong>: each chart instruction commits to two=
 axes, accepting the third as bookkeeping. This is the structural reason th=
ere are so many primitives =E2=80=94 they cover the pairwise combinations o=
f {data, compute, state}.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Two-axis tradeoffs at every move</strong>: each M-move explicitly t=
rades between two of three axes. The third axis is the constraint.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Triadic instruction is unique</strong>: <code class=3D"bg-text-200/=
5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap r=
ounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">interp</code> is the only three-a=
xis primitive. It's the move that <em>closes</em> the system because it eng=
ages all three axes simultaneously.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Fat tree nodes emerged at higher orders</strong>: each aggregation =
level is itself a primitive that engages two axes (typically compute + data=
) and produces a higher-order result.</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">This is the actual structural answer to "where Reed-Muller is in pl=
ay." Not in the hardware specifics or the Hamming codeword analysis =E2=80=
=94 in our <strong>instruction-set design discipline</strong>. Every primit=
ive operates on two of three axes by deliberate choice. The system is self-=
similar at every level: the same two-axis-pick-two pattern repeats from <co=
de class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-0=
00 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">cons</cod=
e> (data + state) to <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-=
border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px =
text-[0.9rem]">apply</code> (compute + state) to <code class=3D"bg-text-200=
/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap =
rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">interp</code> (the closure) to M=
16's search (compute aggregating data) to M22's Walsh-Hadamard (compute dec=
omposing the three orthogonal projections).</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">The architecture is <em>built</em> by repeatedly making the same ki=
nd of move at different scales: pick two axes, design an instruction, accep=
t the third as the constraint. Then aggregate via a fat tree node that pick=
s two axes at the next level up. Repeat.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading=
-[1.7]">That's the structural pattern. The hardware analysis was downstream=
 of it =E2=80=94 answering "how to accelerate this pattern" rather than "wh=
at is this pattern."</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></d=
