verflow: hidden; position: relative;"><p class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap break=
-words">This is strong as a **demonstration harness**, but several claims n=
eed tightening before it can serve as evidence.
The core good idea: <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-b=
order-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px t=
ext-[0.9rem]">default_table</code> as chart data plus <code class=3D"bg-tex=
t-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-=
wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">interp(default_table, term)=
</code> gives you a real self-referential substrate. The grammar is not mer=
ely =E2=80=9Crepresented beside=E2=80=9D the evaluator; it is present as te=
rms consumable by the evaluator. That is the right metacircular shape.
The main correction: the file=E2=80=99s comments oscillate between:
&gt; <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-=
danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">i=
nterp(table, table) is NOT a trivial fixed point</code>
and later synthesis says:
&gt; <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-=
danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">i=
nterp(table, table) is a fixed point</code>
Those cannot both stand. The code says the important thing:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
result =3D c.interp(table, table)
is_fixed =3D c.eq(result, table)
</code>
So the synthesis should say:
&gt; <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-=
danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">i=
nterp(table, table)</code> is generally not a trivial fixed point; iterated=
 interpretation reaches a fixed point/normal form.
That distinction is load-bearing. One-step self-reduction is not the same a=
s self-normalization.
Second issue: Section 2 only proves agreement on selected examples. It does=
 not establish:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
=E2=88=80 term. apply(term) =3D interp(default_table, term)
</code>
It demonstrates agreement on chosen SKI redexes. That is fine, but the text=
 should say =E2=80=9Csampled agreement=E2=80=9D unless you add exhaustive/p=
roperty testing over bounded chart terms.
Third issue: =E2=80=9CEach grammar reduction step is one of the 24 ops=E2=
=80=9D is not actually shown. You record <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 borde=
r border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-=
[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">'apply'</code> and <code class=3D"bg-tex=
t-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-=
wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">'interp'</code> as registry=
 ops, but <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 =
text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9re=
m]">apply(term)</code> internally may perform structural work not decompose=
d into the 24-operation trace. Right now the op trace is an **external anno=
tation trace**, not a proof-carrying operational trace.
The stronger version would make every evaluator primitive emit a witnessed =
op receipt:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
result, receipt =3D c.apply_with_receipt(expr)
</code>
Then the spectral signature is grounded in execution, not post hoc labeling=
.
Fourth issue: <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-=
300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0=
.9rem]">spectral_signature</code> has a small naming mismatch:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
raw =3D [code &amp; 0b10011 for _name, code in trace]
</code>
This keeps bit 4 and bits 0=E2=80=931, dropping pairing bits. That is fine,=
 but the comment says =E2=80=9C3 lowest bits,=E2=80=9D which is false. It i=
s a 3-bit projection from <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 bo=
rder-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 p=
y-px text-[0.9rem]">{chirality, witness}</code>.
Better:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
# Project codeword to chirality bit + 2 witness bits.
raw =3D [code &amp; 0b10011 for _name, code in trace]
</code>
Fifth issue: SPPF sharing is real if <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border bo=
rder-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4=
rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_hashcons</code> canonicalizes <code class=
=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 white=
space-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">(left, right)</co=
de> pairs, but =E2=80=9CSPPF=E2=80=9D may be slightly too strong unless pac=
ked ambiguity/families are represented. What you currently show is **hash-c=
onsed term DAG sharing**. It becomes SPPF-like when multiple derivational w=
itnesses can point to the same structural cell.
So I=E2=80=99d phrase it as:
&gt; Hash-consing provides the DAG-sharing substrate required by an SPPF; p=
acked ambiguity requires witness/family records layered over shared cells.
The deepest architectural point: this module is approaching a three-layer c=
losure:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
term algebra
  =E2=86=93
rule table as term algebra data
  =E2=86=93
interpreter consumes rule-table terms
  =E2=86=93
execution emits op-codeword trace
  =E2=86=93
spectral analysis reads trace as substrate signal
</code>
That is valuable. But to make the claim fully constructible, the receipts n=
eed to be endogenous: the evaluator should not merely be called and then re=
corded; it should produce the witnessed operation trace as part of its sema=
ntics.
The next best refactor is probably:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
@dataclass(frozen=3DTrue)
class EvalReceipt:
    op_name: str
    codeword: int
    before: int
    after: int
    rule: int | None =3D None
    witness: int | None =3D None
</code>
Then define:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
interp_with_trace(table, term) -&gt; tuple[int, list[EvalReceipt]]
apply_with_trace(term) -&gt; tuple[int, list[EvalReceipt]]
normalize_with_trace(term) -&gt; tuple[int, list[EvalReceipt]]
</code>
After that, the key theorem becomes executable:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
For bounded generated terms:
  normalize_with_trace(term).result
    =3D
  iterated_interp_with_trace(default_table, term).result
and both traces project into the 24-op spectral address space.
</code>
In short: the module has the right skeleton. The main thing missing is **re=
ceipt-carrying evaluation**. Without it, the spectral layer is descriptive.=
 With it, the spectral layer becomes semantic evidence.</p></div><button cl=
