ace-y-0 [&amp;_ul]:pl-8 [&amp;_ol]:pl-8" style=3D"max-height: none; overflo=
w: hidden; position: relative;"><p class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap break-words=
">This is much tighter than v3. The important improvement is that the *witn=
ess path is no longer the same morphism as the mutating execution path*. Th=
at makes the claim materially testable.
The core win:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
execute_with_receipt =E2=89=A0 verify_receipt
verify_receipt uses replay
replay does not append receipt
receipt carries enough local data to be checked against replay
</code>
That is the right triangle:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
execution path:   State =E2=94=80=E2=94=80step=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=96=B6 S=
tate + Receipt
replay path:      State =E2=94=80=E2=94=80pure=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=96=B6 S=
tate
verification:     Receipt =C3=97 State =E2=94=80=E2=94=80replay/compare=E2=
=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=96=B6 Bool
</code>
The strongest part is the downgrade of =E2=80=9Cendogenous.=E2=80=9D That h=
onesty matters. This is not yet fully endogenous at the chart primitive lev=
el; it is receipt-native at the wrapper level. Good.
Main issue: <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-30=
0 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9=
rem]">verify_receipt</code> is not fully non-mutating, because <code class=
=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 white=
space-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">apply_replay</cod=
e> / <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-=
danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">i=
nterp_replay</code> may allocate via <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border bo=
rder-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4=
rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">cons</code> / <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 b=
order border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap roun=
ded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_substitute</code>. You acknowledge =
this, but the phrase =E2=80=9Cpure replay=E2=80=9D is therefore only observ=
ationally pure relative to <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 b=
order-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 =
py-px text-[0.9rem]">_history</code> and <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 borde=
r border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-=
[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_apply_memo</code>, not chart-pure.
I would rename the purity class:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
history-pure / memo-pure / trace-pure
</code>
rather than simply:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
pure
</code>
because hash-cons allocation is still state mutation.
The next tightening should be:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
@dataclass(frozen=3DTrue)
class ReplayEffects:
    before_cell_count: int
    after_cell_count: int
    allocated: Tuple[int, ...]
</code>
or, better, add a replay mode that refuses allocation:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
def cons_existing(c, l, r) -&gt; Optional[int]:
    return c._hashcons.get((l, r))
</code>
Then verification can choose:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
strict verification: no allocation allowed
constructive verification: allocation allowed but reported
</code>
That gives you the missing distinction.
The second issue is that interp receipts are table-local but not grammar-ta=
ble-content-local. <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bo=
rder-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px te=
xt-[0.9rem]">r.table</code> records the cell ID of the table, not a digest =
or structural witness of the table=E2=80=99s contents. Within one chart thi=
s is fine. Across charts, =E2=80=9Csame atoms=E2=80=9D is not enough unless=
 cell IDs and cons structure are known stable or remapped.
So the claim:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
off-chart verification with the same atoms
</code>
should probably become:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
off-chart verification against an isomorphic chart image carrying the same =
table cell under the chart isomorphism
</code>
or you need a table digest / canonical encoding.
The third issue: state-mutating receipts are not really verified. They are =
authenticated only at the operation-address layer:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
codeword =3D=3D expected(op_name)
</code>
That proves the receipt names a registered operation. It does not prove the=
 operation occurred, was valid, or produced <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 bo=
rder border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap round=
ed-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">after</code>.
Your own text says this, but I would make the verifier reflect it:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
class VerificationLevel(Enum):
    REPLAY_VERIFIED =3D "replay_verified"
    ADDRESS_VERIFIED =3D "address_verified"
    FAILED =3D "failed"
</code>
Returning <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 =
text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9re=
m]">bool</code> collapses an important distinction. For this project, that =
collapse is dangerous.
Best patch:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
@dataclass(frozen=3DTrue)
class VerificationResult:
    ok: bool
    level: str
    reason: str
</code>
Then:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
apply/interp =E2=86=92 replay_verified
store/evolve/load/etc. =E2=86=92 address_verified_only
</code>
This prevents accidental overclaiming.
The fourth issue is <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-b=
order-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px t=
ext-[0.9rem]">normalize_with_trace</code>: on quiescence it returns <code c=
lass=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 w=
hitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">cur</code>, n=
ot <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">aft=
er</code>.
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
if c.eq(after, cur):
    return cur, receipts
</code>
Equivalent by <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-=
300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0=
.9rem]">eq</code>, so probably harmless, but semantically the trace says th=
e final cursor is <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bor=
der-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px tex=
t-[0.9rem]">after</code>. Better:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
if c.eq(after, cur):
    return after, receipts
</code>
Same for <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 t=
ext-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem=
]">iterated_interp_with_trace</code>.
The fifth issue: tampering with <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-=
0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] =
px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">after ^ 1</code> may accidentally produce a valid=
 but weird cell ID, or an invalid one, depending on chart size. It is okay =
as a smoke test, but not as a robust tamper test. Better choose a known dif=
ferent valid cell:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
tampered_after =3D c.TRUE if not c.eq(bad.after, c.TRUE) else c.FALSE
</code>
The categorical claim is now mostly right, but I would phrase it like this:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
For replay-verifiable evaluator operations, the evaluator is lifted from a =
partial state transition
    State =E2=87=80 State
to a witnessed transition
    State =E2=87=80 =CE=A3 after : State . Receipt(before, after)
where verification is a separate morphism
    Verify : State =C3=97 Receipt =E2=86=92 Bool
that recomputes the transition through a replay path observationally indepe=
ndent of receipt emission.
</code>
That avoids pretending all operations are equally lifted.
The key refinement:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
WitnessedArrow(State, State)
</code>
is slightly too strong for the whole file. More precise:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
ReplayWitnessedArrow for apply/interp
AddressWitnessedArrow for mutating workspace ops
</code>
or:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
Receipt has a verification stratum.
</code>
Overall judgment: this is a real architectural advance. The replay split ma=
kes the proof-carrying story operational instead of decorative. The remaini=
ng gap is not conceptual; it is classification. You need to stop returning =
undifferentiated <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bord=
er-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text=
-[0.9rem]">bool</code> verification, because the system now has at least th=
ree observably distinct verification levels:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
replay-verified
address-verified-only
failed
</code>
