This is a real improvement. The key gain is that v5 stops pretending =E2=80=
=9Cverified=E2=80=9D is one predicate. It has separated:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">receip=
t names a registered operation</code>
from
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">receip=
t is observationally replayable from before to after</code>.
That distinction is load-bearing.
The strongest part is this:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
VerificationResult(ok, level, reason)
</code>
because it prevents the verifier from collapsing heterogeneous evidence int=
o <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dan=
ger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">True=
</code>. That is exactly the right move.
The remaining issue is that the code still has three partially entangled ax=
es:
1. **operation-address validity**
2. **transition replay validity**
3. **chart residency / allocation effects**
Right now you model 1 and 2 explicitly, and describe 3 in prose. The next s=
tructural gain is to make 3 explicit in the return value too.
I would split the current <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 bo=
rder-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 p=
y-px text-[0.9rem]">level</code> into two orthogonal fields:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
transition_level:
    replay_verified | address_verified | failed
purity_level:
    trace_pure | chart_pure | allocating | failed
</code>
Because <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 te=
xt-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]=
">apply_replay</code> and <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 bo=
rder-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 p=
y-px text-[0.9rem]">interp_replay</code> are not merely =E2=80=9Cnot chart-=
pure=E2=80=9D; they are **chart-extending replay witnesses**. That is a leg=
itimate stratum, but it should not be hidden under the same label as a look=
up-only replay.
The most important correction:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
verify_receipt(...)
</code>
is not fully =E2=80=9Cpure=E2=80=9D in the ordinary semantic sense because =
it may allocate cells. Your prose says this correctly, but the function doc=
string says:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
Trace-pure (history-pure + memo-pure). Hash-cons allocation may occur.
</code>
That is fine, but the name <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 b=
order-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 =
py-px text-[0.9rem]">verify_receipt</code> can mislead. It is really:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
verify_receipt_by_replay_or_address(...)
</code>
or, more formally:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
verify_receipt_observationally(...)
</code>
because replay may perturb chart extent.
The cross-chart caveat is also correct, but it implies a stronger design bo=
undary:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
EvalReceipt.table: Optional[int]
</code>
is not globally meaningful evidence. It is a **local pointer witness**, not=
 a portable witness. So <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 bord=
er-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-=
px text-[0.9rem]">EvalReceipt</code> currently has chart-local semantics. A=
 portable version needs one of:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
table_digest: Optional[bytes]
table_root_digest: Optional[bytes]
table_canonical_form: Optional[bytes]
chart_generation_id: Optional[int]
</code>
The cleanest next version is probably:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
@dataclass(frozen=3DTrue)
class LocalCellRef:
    cell_id: int
    chart_id: Optional[str] =3D None
    digest: Optional[bytes] =3D None
</code>
