den; position: relative;"><p class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">This=
 is a real upgrade. v6 correctly stops saying =E2=80=9Cverified=E2=80=9D as=
 one scalar and instead reports a product judgment:
[
V =3D T \times P \times L
]
transition proof =C3=97 verifier effects =C3=97 reference scope.
The strongest parts:
1. **Three axes are the right decomposition.** Replay, purity, and locality=
 are not refinements of one another; they are independent witnesses.
2. **Registry digest is the right kind of environment witness.** It prevent=
s a receipt from silently verifying under a changed address space.
3. **Mixed-stratum downgrade is conceptually correct.** <code class=3D"bg-t=
ext-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pr=
e-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">apply + store</code> shou=
ld not inherit replay-level proof just because one step replayed.
4. **=E2=80=9CChart-extending verification=E2=80=9D is honestly reported.**=
 This is much better than pretending replay is pure merely because it avoid=
s <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dan=
ger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_his=
tory</code> and <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-borde=
r-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-=
[0.9rem]">_apply_memo</code>.
Main problems:
### 1. <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 tex=
t-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]"=
>FAILED_PURITY</code> currently means too much
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">Verifi=
cationResult.fail()</code> always returns:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
purity_level=3DFAILED_PURITY
locality=3DFAILED_LOCALITY
</code>
But most failures are not purity failures. A bad codeword, bad registry dig=
est, or replay mismatch does not imply the verifier mutated <code class=3D"=
bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespac=
e-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_history</code> or <c=
ode class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-=
000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_apply_m=
emo</code>.
Better:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
@dataclass(frozen=3DTrue)
class VerificationResult:
    ok: bool
    transition_level: str
    purity_level: str
    locality: str
    reason: str
</code>
Failure should preserve observed purity/locality when possible:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
@classmethod
def fail(cls, reason: str, *,
         purity_level: str =3D CHART_PURE,
         locality: str =3D CHART_LOCAL) -&gt; 'VerificationResult':
    return cls(False, FAILED, purity_level, locality, reason)
</code>
Reserve <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 te=
xt-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]=
">FAILED_PURITY</code> only for detected verifier mutation.
### 2. Purity is claimed, not checked
The doc says <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-3=
00 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.=
9rem]">FAILED_PURITY</code> covers <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border bord=
er-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4re=
m] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_history</code> or <code class=3D"bg-text-200/=
5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap r=
ounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_apply_memo</code> mutation, but =
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">verify=
_receipt()</code> never snapshots them.
Add an observer wrapper:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
def _observe_verification_effects(c, thunk):
    hist_before =3D len(c._history)
    memo_before =3D dict(c._apply_memo)
    cells_before =3D len(c._cells)
    result =3D thunk()
    cells_after =3D len(c._cells)
    hist_after =3D len(c._history)
    memo_after =3D dict(c._apply_memo)
    if hist_after !=3D hist_before or memo_after !=3D memo_before:
        return result, FAILED_PURITY, cells_after - cells_before
    purity =3D CHART_PURE if cells_after =3D=3D cells_before else CHART_EXT=
ENDING
    return result, purity, cells_after - cells_before
</code>
Then replay verification becomes actually effect-audited.
### 3. Registry digest detects address drift, not semantic drift
This digest:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
(op.name, encode_op(op).code)
</code>
detects rename/removal/codeword movement. It does **not** detect changed im=
plementation semantics if the same op name/codeword now points to different=
 behavior.
So the claim should be narrowed:
&gt; registry_digest detects registry address-image drift, not operational =
semantic drift.
To detect semantic drift, include an implementation witness, for example:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
(op.name, encode_op(op).code, op.semantic_digest)
</code>
or, weaker but useful:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
(op.name, encode_op(op).code, op.kind, op.arity, op.version)
</code>
### 4. Chart-local replay proves only =E2=80=9Cunder this chart image=E2=80=
=9D
This is acknowledged, but it is important: raw IDs make receipts intensiona=
l to the current chart object. If two charts assign <code class=3D"bg-text-=
200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wr=
ap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">#17</code> differently, the s=
ame receipt may replay a different statement.
So the categorical statement should be sharpened:
&gt; A chart-local receipt does not identify a transition absolutely. It id=
entifies a transition relative to the pair <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 bor=
der border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounde=
d-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">(chart identity, registry image)</code=
>. Replay verifies compatibility with that environment, not portable equali=
ty of transitions.
### 5. <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 tex=
t-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]"=
>verify_trace()</code> mutates the chart during verification
Because <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 te=
xt-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]=
">verify_receipt()</code> may allocate cells, the act of verifying receipt =
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">i</cod=
e> can change the environment for receipt <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 bord=
er border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded=
-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">i+1</code>.
That is not necessarily unsound, but it means trace verification is not a p=
ure fold over a fixed chart image. It is a fold over a verifier-extended ch=
art image.
You should explicitly name this:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
verify_trace is monotone environment-extending verification, not frozen-ima=
ge verification.
</code>
The future <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300=
 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9r=
em]">cons_existing</code> lookup-only mode is therefore not just an optimiz=
ation; it changes the theorem.
### 6. Address-verified state ops are barely verified
For state-mutating ops, this proves only:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
op_name =E2=86=94 codeword under current registry
</code>
It does not prove:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
store(w, x) actually stored x at w
load(w) actually returned the stored value
validated_store respected pred
workspace_witness witnessed anything
</code>
That is fine, but the name <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 b=
order-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 =
py-px text-[0.9rem]">address_verified</code> is exactly right. Do not let l=
ater prose call these =E2=80=9Cstate verified.=E2=80=9D
A next tier could be:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
EFFECT_VERIFIED
</code>
for receipts that carry enough pre/post state digest to prove state transit=
ion.
### 7. The registry digest may be over-strict
Adding an unrelated op changes the digest and invalidates old receipts, eve=
n if the old op=E2=80=99s address did not change.
That may be desired if the registry image is part of the environment witnes=
s. But there are two possible notions:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
global_registry_digest: whole op table unchanged
op_address_digest: this op=E2=80=99s name/codeword unchanged
</code>
Current v6 uses the stronger one. Good for reproducibility; too strict for =
incremental compatibility.
Recommended next step:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
v7 should split environment witness into:
  registry_global_digest
  op_entry_digest
  chart_image_digest / local cell witnesses
  verifier_effect_report
</code>
My compact verdict:
&gt; v6 is structurally sound as a verification stratification, but not yet=
 sound as an effect-audited verifier. The conceptual lattice is right; the =
implementation still collapses ordinary failure into purity failure, does n=
ot actually observe history/memo mutation, and treats registry-address stab=
ility as stronger than it really is. The next leverage point is not portabi=
lity yet; it is making purity observationally true.</p></div><button class=
