e; overflow: hidden; position: relative;"><p class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap b=
reak-words">This is a real v7, not a cosmetic bump. The main repair is soun=
d: **failure is no longer conflated with impurity**, and purity is now obse=
rvational instead of declarative.
The strongest parts:
1. **Three axes are finally orthogonal enough**
   * <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-=
danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">t=
ransition_level</code>: did the transition verify?
   * <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-=
danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">p=
urity_level</code>: what did verification do to the chart?
   * <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-=
danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">l=
ocality</code>: what environment is the receipt meaningful in?
2. **<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-=
danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">F=
AILED_PURITY</code> now means =E2=80=9Cverifier mutated forbidden state=E2=
=80=9D**
   That is the right semantics. A bad receipt should be false, not impure.
3. **<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-=
danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">o=
p_address_digest</code> is the right refinement**
   Global <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 =
text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9re=
m]">registry_digest</code> is too strong for incremental systems. Per-op ad=
dress stability is the constructible witness you actually need.
4. **The categorical statement is honest**
   Receipts are not portable proofs. They are proofs relative to:
   <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">tex=
t
   chart identity =C3=97 registry image =C3=97 op-address image
   </code>
   That is the correct object.
Remaining leaks:
### 1. <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 tex=
t-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]"=
>_observe_verification_effects</code> observes only lengths
This catches append/growth, but not mutation-in-place.
For example, these would evade detection:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
c._history[0] =3D ...
c._apply_memo[k] =3D different_value
c._cells[i] =3D different_cell
</code>
So v7 observes **extent purity**, not full structural purity.
Better name:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
EXTENT_PURE
EXTENT_EXTENDING
FAILED_EXTENT_PURITY
</code>
Or strengthen the snapshot with structural digests.
### 2. Exceptions inside <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 bor=
der-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py=
-px text-[0.9rem]">thunk()</code> bypass effect reporting
If replay mutates and then raises, the mutation is not reported.
Use <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-d=
anger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">tr=
y/finally</code>-style observation:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
try:
    result =3D thunk()
    error =3D None
except Exception as e:
    result =3D None
    error =3D e
finally:
    observe_after()
</code>
Then return either <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bo=
rder-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px te=
xt-[0.9rem]">(result, purity, allocated, error)</code> or raise an audited =
exception.
### 3. <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 tex=
t-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]"=
>c.eq(...)</code> after replay is outside the purity audit
In <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">ver=
ify_receipt</code>, the audited region is only:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
lambda: apply_replay(c, r.before)
</code>
But then this happens outside the audit:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
if not c.eq(recomputed, r.after):
</code>
If <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">eq<=
/code> can allocate, memoize, normalize, or log, purity reporting is incomp=
lete.
The audited thunk should include the whole verification kernel:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
def kernel():
    recomputed =3D apply_replay(c, r.before)
    matched =3D c.eq(recomputed, r.after)
    return recomputed, matched
</code>
Same for interp.
### 4. <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 tex=
t-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]"=
>registry_digest</code> still dominates <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border=
 border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[=
0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">op_address_digest</code>
This is intentional in the code:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
if r.registry_digest is not None:
    check global first
</code>
But it slightly contradicts the =E2=80=9Cless-strict alternative=E2=80=9D s=
tory. Current semantics are:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
no digest            =E2=86=92 legacy permissive
op digest only       =E2=86=92 per-op compatible
registry digest only =E2=86=92 globally compatible
both digests         =E2=86=92 globally compatible AND per-op compatible
</code>
That should be stated explicitly.
### 5. Address verification is still very weak
You already say this, but the implementation makes it stark:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
return address_ok(...)
</code>
For state-mutating ops, this verifies only:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
op_name =E2=86=94 codeword
</code>
Not:
* that the op ran,
* that <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 tex=
t-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]"=
>before</code> was consumed,
* that <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 tex=
t-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]"=
>after</code> was produced,
* that the mutation occurred,
* that the effect was legal.
So the next axis probably wants to be:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
effect_level:
    EFFECT_REPLAY_VERIFIED
    EFFECT_RECEIPT_DECLARED
    EFFECT_UNVERIFIED
    FAILED_EFFECT
</code>
### 6. Chart identity remains implicit
This is acceptable for v7, but it is the largest deferred obligation. A rec=
eipt whose <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300=
 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9r=
em]">before</code>/<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bo=
rder-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px te=
xt-[0.9rem]">after</code> are integer cell IDs is not merely chart-local; i=
t is **chart-instance-local**.
Eventually <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300=
 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9r=
em]">EvalReceipt</code> wants something like:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
chart_digest: Optional[str]
cell_before_digest: Optional[str]
cell_after_digest: Optional[str]
</code>
Until then, <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-30=
0 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9=
rem]">PORTABLE</code> should remain unreachable.
The clean v8 thesis:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
v7 audits verifier extent effects.
v8 audits verifier structural effects.
v9 audits state-operation effects.
v10 introduces portable cell/table digests.
</code>
My verdict: **v7 is coherent and meaningfully stronger than v6**, but its p=
urity claim should be narrowed to =E2=80=9Cno observed growth/memo-history =
append=E2=80=9D unless you upgrade snapshots from lengths to structural dig=
ests.</p></div><button class=3D"pb-3 pt-1 text-xs text-text-500/80 hover:te=
