verflow: hidden; position: relative;"><p class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap break=
-words">This is a real improvement over v8. The main achievement is that v9=
 stops pretending one verifier result is one scalar. It turns verification =
into a product object:
[
V =3D T \times P \times L \times E \times \mathbb{N}
]
where transition, purity, locality, effect, and allocation are independentl=
y observable.
The strongest fixes are:
1. **<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-=
danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">R=
EPLAY_VERIFIED</code> is now intensional**
   Good. <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 t=
ext-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem=
]">recomputed =3D=3D r.after</code> and <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border=
 border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[=
0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">c.eq(recomputed, r.after)</code> are diff=
erent claims. Even if currently collapsed, the code now preserves the futur=
e distinction.
2. **Chart-instance locality is no longer merely prose**
   The nonce is not portable, but it is an actual witness. That is the righ=
t move for v9.
3. **Address verification is visibly weak**
   <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">ADD=
RESS_VERIFIED + EFFECT_UNVERIFIED</code> is much better than pretending cod=
eword agreement verifies the effect.
4. **Verifier purity is observable**
   <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_ob=
serve_verification_effects</code> gives a concrete behavioral test: did ver=
ification allocate, mutate history, mutate memo, or change cells?
The main remaining issue: **the nonce implementation has an identity-reuse =
bug.**
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
_chart_nonces: Dict[int, str] =3D {}
key =3D id(c)
</code>
If a chart is garbage-collected and Python reuses the object id, the old no=
nce may be incorrectly assigned to a new chart. The docstring admits this, =
but the fix is easy enough that I would not defer it.
Use weak references:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
import weakref
_chart_nonces =3D weakref.WeakKeyDictionary()
def compute_chart_instance_nonce(c: ChartChained) -&gt; str:
    if c not in _chart_nonces:
        _chart_nonces[c] =3D uuid.uuid4().hex
    return _chart_nonces[c]
</code>
If <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">Cha=
rtChained</code> is not weak-referenceable, add <code class=3D"bg-text-200/=
5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap r=
ounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">__weakref__</code> to its slots o=
r wrap with a sidecar object.
Second major issue: **<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border=
-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px=
 text-[0.9rem]">interp_replay</code> is not actually pure if it calls <code=
 class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000=
 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">c.cons</cod=
e>.**
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
return c.cons(new_l, c.right(term))
</code>
Even during verification, this can allocate. v9 detects that as <code class=
=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 white=
space-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">CHART_EXTENDING</=
code>, which is good, but semantically this means replay is not pure replay=
; it is replay-with-possible-reconstruction. That is acceptable only if <co=
de class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-0=
00 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">CHART_EXT=
ENDING</code> is treated as weaker than <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border=
 border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[=
0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">CHART_PURE</code>, which you do. But the =
name =E2=80=9Cpure replay paths=E2=80=9D is slightly false.
A better v10 target is already named correctly: <code class=3D"bg-text-200/=
5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap r=
ounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">cons_existing</code>. Verificatio=
n should either:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
return c.cons_existing(new_l, c.right(term))
</code>
or fail with =E2=80=9Cmissing reconstructed cell=E2=80=9D rather than alloc=
ate.
Third issue: **<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border=
-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[=
0.9rem]">store_with_receipt</code> has confusing before/after polarity.**
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
def store_with_receipt(c, w_id, data_id):
    c.store(w_id, data_id)
    return w_id, _state_receipt(c, 'store', data_id, w_id)
</code>
This says <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 =
text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9re=
m]">before=3Ddata_id</code>, <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5=
 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-=
1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">after=3Dw_id</code>. But the state operation is not =
really a transition from <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 bor=
der-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py=
-px text-[0.9rem]">data_id</code> to <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border bo=
rder-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4=
rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">w_id</code>; it is a mutation of chart/works=
pace state. So the transition axis for state ops is structurally bogus unle=
ss you explicitly define =E2=80=9Clogical result transition=E2=80=9D separa=
tely from =E2=80=9Cstate effect transition.=E2=80=9D
For v10, I would split receipt fields:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
input_id: Optional[int]
output_id: Optional[int]
state_pre_digest: Optional[str]
state_post_digest: Optional[str]
</code>
Then state ops do not fake a term transition.
Fourth issue: **digesting with <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0=
.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] p=
x-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">repr</code> is still a semantic footgun.**
You already name this. The problem is not just collisions or truncation. It=
 is that <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 t=
ext-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem=
]">repr</code> can be:
* process-dependent,
* address-bearing,
* order-sensitive through nested structures,
* version-sensitive,
* not canonical for user-defined objects.
So v10 canonical byte encoding should be first-class, not cosmetic.
The most important v10 dependency order should be:
1. <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">con=
s_existing</code> / no-allocation replay.
2. canonical byte encoding.
3. chart/cell digests.
4. state-op pre/post digests.
5. portable receipts.
6. semantic op digests.
The deeper architectural correction:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">Verifi=
cationResult</code> is no longer merely a result. It is a **graded proof ob=
