## What is solid
The best parts are:
1. **M38/M40 distinction is named honestly.**
   The codeword space and spectral closure have same cardinality but differ=
ent group structure. You explicitly prevent the false inference:
   <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">tex=
t
   same 24 labels =E2=87=92 same algebra
   </code>
   Good. That is exactly the bug an LLM or future maintainer would collapse=
.
2. **Receipt sum types are a real structural improvement.**
   <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">Ter=
mReceipt</code>, <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bord=
er-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text=
-[0.9rem]">StateReceipt</code>, and <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border bor=
der-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4r=
em] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">ObservationReceipt</code> eliminate the forge=
able <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-=
danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">t=
ransition_kind</code> field. This is a genuine runtime distinction: constru=
ctible, reachable, observable, and covered.
3. **Fail-closed canonical encoding is good.**
   <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_ca=
nonical_bytes</code> refusing unknown types is correct. It makes digest pur=
ity meaningful instead of advisory.
4. **The codeword/address bijection is actually exhaustive.**
   <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">ver=
ify_codeword_address_bijection()</code> checks both directions and invalid =
codes. That is a real finite theorem.
5. **<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-=
danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">C=
HART_LOCAL</code> is finally honest.**
   The nonce says =E2=80=9Csame live object lineage,=E2=80=9D not =E2=80=9C=
same denotation.=E2=80=9D That is the right locality grade.
## Main weakness
The theorem name overstates:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
verify_m41_grammar_is_well_typed_and_admissible(c)
</code>
It verifies:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">text
some local construction invariants + one live receipt example
</code>
It does **not** verify the grammar is globally well-typed or globally admis=
sible.
A more precise name would be:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
verify_m41_receipt_kernel_admissibility(c)
</code>
or:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
verify_m41_address_and_receipt_invariants(c)
</code>
The current theorem proves the **receipt/address verification scaffold** is=
 coherent, not the whole applied grammar.
## Biggest concrete bug / mismatch
This comment says v13 adds replay for state effects:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
