ol]:pl-8" style=3D"max-height: none; overflow: hidden; position: relative;"=
><p class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">This is a real v17 closure, b=
ut one invariant is still dangerously false:
**=E2=80=9CFail-closed verification does not perturb the chart.=E2=80=9D**
It detects perturbation, but does not undo it.
The biggest issue is here:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
replay_value, purity, allocated, error =3D _observe_verification_effects(c,=
 replay_thunk)
</code>
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_obser=
ve_verification_effects</code> snapshots, observes, classifies, and returns=
. It does **not restore**. So a mutating <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 borde=
r border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-=
[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">spec.replay</code> is caught as <code cl=
ass=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 wh=
itespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">FAILED_PURITY =
+ FAILED_EFFECT</code>, but the chart has already been mutated. Same issue =
exists in permissive term replay: if strict replay misses, <code class=3D"b=
g-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace=
-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_observe_verification_=
effects(c, kernel)</code> may allocate, and then verification can fail with=
 <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dang=
er-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">allow=
_extending=3DFalse</code> after the mutation already occurred.
So v17 makes the verifier stop **lying**, but it does not yet make the veri=
fier **non-destructive**.
The v18 correction should be a transactional verification boundary:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
def _transactional_observe(c, thunk):
    snap =3D _deep_snapshot_mutable_chart(c)
    before =3D _snapshot_chart_state(c)
    try:
        result =3D thunk()
        error =3D None
    except Exception as e:
        result =3D None
        error =3D e
    after =3D _snapshot_chart_state(c)
    purity, allocated =3D _classify_effect(before, after, c)
    _restore_mutable_chart(c, snap)
    return result, purity, allocated, error
</code>
But the snapshot must include more than <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border=
 border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[=
0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_history</code>, <code class=3D"bg-text-2=
00/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wra=
p rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_apply_memo</code>, and <code =
class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 =
whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_cells</code=
>. It likely must include at least:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
_cells
_hashcons
_apply_memo
_history
_atoms
workspace/storage structures
any registry mutation surface, if mutable
</code>
Otherwise purity is only =E2=80=9Cstate-digest pure,=E2=80=9D not =E2=80=9C=
chart-object pure.=E2=80=9D
Second issue: <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-=
300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0=
.9rem]">compute_chart_state_digest</code> and <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 =
border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rou=
nded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_snapshot_chart_state</code> define=
 different state surfaces. The digest includes:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
_history
_apply_memo
_cells
</code>
The structural snapshot includes separate digests and lengths for those sam=
e three fields. But replay purity depends on all mutable fields that affect=
 later execution. If <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-=
border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px =
text-[0.9rem]">_hashcons</code> changes without <code class=3D"bg-text-200/=
5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap r=
ounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_cells</code> changing, the chart=
=E2=80=99s future behavior may change while purity still reports clean.
Third issue: v17 says every receipt=E2=80=99s codeword can decompose orbit-=
canonically, but the verifier does not yet enforce that bridge. <code class=
=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 white=
space-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">_check_codeword_c=
onsistency</code> verifies registry codeword validity, but not:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
codeword_to_signature(r.codeword)
codeword_to_orbit_decomposition(r.codeword)
signature_to_codeword(sig) =3D=3D r.codeword
</code>
So the bridge exists as a library invariant, not yet as a receipt verificat=
ion obligation.
The clean v18 move is:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
@dataclass(frozen=3DTrue)
class ContentAddressedReceiptFields:
    signature: Signature
    orbit_key: OrbitKey
    v4_delta: str
    orbit_canonical_digest: str
</code>
and then every receipt construction should derive these fields from <code c=
lass=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 w=
hitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">codeword</cod=
e>, while every verifier checks:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
sig =3D codeword_to_signature(r.codeword)
decomp =3D decompose_signature(sig)
r.signature =3D=3D sig
r.orbit_key =3D=3D decomp.orbit_key
r.v4_delta =3D=3D decomp.v4_delta
r.orbit_canonical_digest =3D=3D digest(decomp.orbit_key)
</code>
That makes the v17 bridge load-bearing.
One smaller semantic concern: the comments around <code class=3D"bg-text-20=
0/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap=
 rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">codeword_to_signature</code> sa=
y pairing identifies the pair containing <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 borde=
r border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-=
[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">(source, sink)</code>, but the implement=
ation does:
<code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dange=
r-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">python
pair1, pair2 =3D PAIRINGS[pairing]
other_pair =3D pair2 if witness in pair1 else pair1
</code>
So the encoded pairing appears to identify the **partition**, while the wit=
ness selects which side is not source/sink. That may be correct, but the pr=
ose should be tightened because this is exactly where future code will drif=
t.
My verdict: v17 is a good audit-fix release, but not yet a portability rele=
ase. It establishes the orbit-canonical decomposition and closes the =E2=80=
=9Cverifier cannot lie=E2=80=9D gap. The next critical step is stronger: **=
verification must be observationally pure, not merely purity-reporting.**</=
